    {
        "content": "<p>This actually surprises me. I thought that the projections were always definable from the recursor, but that is not the case here. On first thought this extra strength seems a little dangerous, but it's probably fine. <code>PSigma'</code> is only a <code>Prop</code> if both arguments are <code>Prop</code>s, and then <code>PSigma'.rec'</code> is just subsingleton elimination.</p>",
        "id": 599850305,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1780503772
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"111080\">Floris van Doorn</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/PSigma.20and.20universe.20levels/near/599850305\">schrieb</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I thought that the projections were always definable from the recursor, but that is not the case here</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, this is <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/7637\">lean4#7637</a>. To quote <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110049\">@Mario Carneiro</span> from <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/9886#issuecomment-3217016527\">here</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The problem is that the projections still exist even though the recursor doesn't. If projections were banned as well on such types then there would be no problem, but as-is the projections don't have a clean metatheoretic explanation. (\"Borderline unsound\" means it goes beyond the bounds of the soundness proof, but I don't actually have a proof of false. It makes soundness an open question.)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So it would be convenient to have it be more general but we aren't sure whether that is sound.</p>",
        "id": 599932700,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1780523128
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is it then already the case that we're not sure if Aaron's code above is sound?</p>",
        "id": 599939254,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1780525601
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, I think. But I'm not exactly sure of the details. I can at least think of an informal argument why Lean would stay sound with this extension (or really, in its current form), that is allowing <code>inductive</code>s with a universe <code>Sort u</code> that is neither always zero or never zero, contains exactly one constructor where the universe of each field is <code>&lt;= u</code> :<br>\nAssume we have a proof <code>lemma unsound : False := ...</code> without universe parameters (if there is one with universe parameters, just set them all to zero). Furthermore, we can assume that the proof doesn't refer to any definitions or theorems (otherwise just unfold them). So the only constants the proof may refer to are axioms, inductives, constructors, recursors and quotient primitives. We only need to do something to \"problematic\" declarations that indirectly refer to <code>Sort</code>-polymorphic inductives (i.e. inductives in a <code>Sort u</code> universe where <code>u</code> is neither never zero or always zero), and the standard axioms and quotient primitives don't refer to those, so we only need to consider inductives, constructors and recursors.<br>\nNow our goal is to, starting from an expression without universe parameters, recursively eliminate universe parameters from problematic declarations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When we encounter a constant reference to a problematic inductive <code>MyInductive.{u1, ..., un}</code> (where <code>u1</code>, ..., <code>un</code> are constant universes), create a new inductive <code>MyInductive_u1_..._un</code> (if it doesn't already exist) without universe parameters where the type is the original type of <code>MyInductive</code> with the universe parameters instantiated to <code>u1</code>, ..., <code>un</code> and problematic references recursively eliminated and the constructors are the original constructors of <code>MyInductive</code> with the universe parameters instantiated to <code>u1</code>, ..., <code>un</code> and problematic references recursively eliminated. Finally, replace the reference to <code>MyInductive.{u1, ..., un}</code> with <code>MyInductive_u1_...un</code>.</li>\n<li>When we encounter a constant reference to a problematic constructor <code>MyInductive.myCtor.{u1, ..., un}</code> of <code>MyInductive.{u1, ..., un}</code>, make sure we already eliminated <code>MyInductive.{u1, ..., un}</code> as described above and then just replace the reference with <code>MyInductive_u1_..._un.myCtor</code></li>\n<li>When we encounter a constant reference to a problematic recursor <code>MyInductive.rec.{elimUniv, u1, ..., un}</code> of <code>MyInductive.{u1, ..., un}</code>, make sure we already eliminated <code>MyInductive.{u1, ..., un}</code> as described above and then just replace the reference with <code>MyInductive_u1_..._un.rec.{elimUniv}</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>I'm pretty sure that this transformation preserves type-correctness and transforms every <code>Sort</code>-polymorphic inductive into (potentially multiple) unproblematic inductives but I might be wrong.</p>",
        "id": 600076394,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1780576614
    }
]